// AMPERE SMpro ACCESS IS A PLATFORM CAPABILITY, not a controller fact and not a MegaRAC reading.
//
// SMpro is the Ampere SoC's own out-of-band register interface (the master-write-read path into the
// RAS error registers). It is present on Ampere Altra-family silicon and ABSENT on any other
// machine: an x86 board has no SMpro to read, so the capability is ABSENT for it, not an empty
// reading and not a refused one. That distinction is the point of this owner — a machine whose
// platform does not carry SMpro is still a valid controlled machine, and the absence must be readable
// as a platform fact rather than mistaken for a failed probe.
//
// The reading itself is re-homed here from the boot diagnostic bundle (gunbc.machine_intake
// .mtcollins1_boot_diagnostic_bundle) because the bundle's own SmproReading is consumed by more than
// the bundle: the MegaRAC boot diagnostic bundle carries an SMpro field, and it must not mint the
// type in its own public surface. Both the bundle and the MegaRAC module import the type from here.
// The raw pass observation it wraps still comes from the observation module this reads through.
//
// NO PLAUSIBLE OUTPUT IS FABRICATED (DESIGN.md §5). An earlier version of this module keyed the
// standing on the platform ARCHITECTURE: `Aarch64 => Present`. That fabricated an output for every
// ARM machine that is not Ampere silicon — an AWS Graviton or an NVIDIA Grace board is aarch64 with
// no SMpro interface at all — and it turned a machine with NO reading into
// `Absent { platform: "the SMpro pass was not taken" }`, which asserts the platform carries SMpro and
// the pass was merely skipped when the truth is it never carried it. A missing reading is not evidence
// about the facility. The facility is a fact about the board's SoC FAMILY, read from the bound board's
// SoC edge (gunbc.host.board_platform_facts) against the cited carrying set in extdeps.cpu.ampere,
// and the architecture is not its subject.
//
// THE KEY IS THE TYPED SoC FAMILY — not a free string (that would be a second, untyped authority
// beside extdeps.cpu.types.SoCFamily) and not an architecture (wrong subject, above). And
// UNESTABLISHED IS ITS OWN ARM, NEVER COLLAPSED INTO NOT_CARRIED: "the board admits no SoC family, so
// nothing is known about the silicon" is not "the family lacks SMpro". Collapsing those two is exactly
// the defect the architecture key produced.
module gunbc.host.smpro_platform_capability

import std.types { Bool, Int, List, NonEmptyStr, String }
import gunbc.machine_intake.ampere_smpro_observation {
  SmproPassObservation, SmproPassSubject, SmproSiteRefusal, SmproSiteStanding, SmproSiteAdmitted,
  SmproSiteRefused,
}
import extdeps.bmc.endpoint { BmcControllerEndpoint }
import extdeps.cpu.types { SoCFamily }
import extdeps.cpu.ampere { ampere_soc_family_carries_smpro }
import extdeps.bmc.access_profile { BmcAccessObservationStanding }
import gunbc.host.board_platform_facts { host_board_soc_families }

// AN SMpro READING: the pass that was taken (every probe kept whole with its raw observation,
// including when none of them was answered), or the typed fact that no pass was taken and why.
// The platform capability below stands on top of this reading. These three names — SmproReading,
// SmproProbed, SmproNotTaken — are constructed by the boot diagnostic bundles and their witnesses and
// by mtcollins1's row; they record WHETHER A PASS WAS TAKEN, which is orthogonal to whether the
// platform could carry one, and they are kept exactly as they are.
type SmproReading
  = SmproProbed { observation: SmproPassObservation }
  | SmproNotTaken { reason: NonEmptyStr }

// THE STANDING OF THE SMpro FACILITY ON A HOST'S PLATFORM. Three answers, and they are genuinely
// three: the family is named and carries the facility; the family is named and does not; the family
// is not established at all.
type SmproFacilityStanding
  = // The board's SoC family is one of the Ampere families the upstream cites as carrying SMpro
    // (extdeps.cpu.ampere.ampere_smpro_carrying_soc_families, cited to
    // extdeps.ampere.altra_ssif_patch). This is what licenses an SMpro read on the platform — it says
    // nothing yet about whether a reading was taken; SmproReading carries that.
    SmproFacilityCarried { family: SoCFamily }
  // The board's SoC family is named and is NOT among the cited carrying families: a real family,
  // negatively answered by the cited set. An x86 machine answers here — and still binds its BMC
  // control route and remains a valid controlled machine, because this fact is not a precondition of
  // control.
  | SmproFacilityNotCarried { family: SoCFamily }
  // The host's board admits no SoC family, so NOTHING is known about the silicon. Not Carried, and
  // not Carried either: there is no family to answer about. The SMpro pass is not taken, and the
  // reason says the family is unknown rather than claiming the platform lacks the facility.
  | SmproFacilityUnestablished { reason: NonEmptyStr }

// HOW A BOARD'S ADMITTED FAMILIES SPLIT AGAINST THE CITED CARRYING SET. Two counts, not one boolean,
// because three situations have to stay distinguishable: every admitted family carries, none does, and
// the roster MIXES the two. The third is not a yes and not a no — it is the compatibility roster saying
// "this board may ship with silicon that has the interface and silicon that does not", which answers
// nothing about the machine in front of us.
type SmproAdmittedCounts {
  total: Int,
  carrying: Int,
}

// THE SMpro FACILITY OF A MACHINE, READ FROM ITS BOUND BOARD'S SoC FAMILY (through
// gunbc.host.board_platform_facts, so the board authority stays the owner of the silicon) AND THE
// UPSTREAM-CITED CARRYING SET. Never from the architecture: aarch64 does not carry SMpro by being
// aarch64, and the answer for an aarch64 family the cited set does not name is NotCarried.
//
// The access observation is the input because it is what binds a host to its board identity; the
// board, not the controller, is the hardware authority.
fn smpro_facility(access: BmcAccessObservationStanding) -> SmproFacilityStanding {
  match host_board_soc_families(access: access) {
    Absent => SmproFacilityUnestablished { reason: "the host has no board row bound to it, so the platform is unknown" }
    Present { value: fs } => smpro_facility_of_families(families: fs)
  }
}

// THE SAME VERDICT OVER AN EXPLICIT FAMILY SET — one implementation, reached two ways. The
// production path above resolves the families through the board authority; this one takes them
// directly, so a witness can ask about a family (an aarch64 family the cited set does not name, a
// family the set names, and no family at all) without minting a board row per question.
fn smpro_facility_of_families(families: List<SoCFamily>) -> SmproFacilityStanding {
  let c = smpro_admitted_counts(families: families)
  if c.total == 0 {
    // NO family at all: nothing is known about the silicon. Unknown, NOT a negative about some
    // family — the collapse the architecture key produced.
    SmproFacilityUnestablished { reason: "the bound board admits no SoC family, so the platform is unknown" }
  } else if c.carrying == c.total {
    // UNANIMOUS YES: whatever is in the slot, it carries the interface.
    match first_soc_family(families: families) {
      Present { value: lead } => SmproFacilityCarried { family: lead }
      Absent => SmproFacilityUnestablished { reason: "the bound board admits no SoC family, so the platform is unknown" }
    }
  } else if c.carrying == 0 {
    // UNANIMOUS NO: every family the board admits is negatively answered by the cited set.
    match first_soc_family(families: families) {
      Present { value: lead } => SmproFacilityNotCarried { family: lead }
      Absent => SmproFacilityUnestablished { reason: "the bound board admits no SoC family, so the platform is unknown" }
    }
  } else {
    // MIXED ROSTER. The board's edge is a COMPATIBILITY roster -- what may be installed -- so it does
    // not say which silicon is in the slot, and no SMpro question about THIS machine is answerable from
    // it. Not Carried (the roster does contain carrying silicon) and not Carried (it also contains
    // silicon that has no such interface). An installed-family observation would settle it; none exists
    // in the corpus yet, so the honest answer is that we do not know -- never the optimistic arm.
    SmproFacilityUnestablished {
      reason: "the bound board admits both SMpro-carrying and non-carrying SoC families and no installed-silicon observation selects one, so this machine's platform is unknown",
    }
  }
}

// THE ADMITTED FAMILIES SPLIT BY THE CITED CARRYING SET, as a count rather than a boolean. The board's
// SoC edge bounds what MAY be installed -- a compatibility roster -- so it is not evidence about which
// silicon is IN the slot. Answering Carried because ANY rostered family carries SMpro would license an
// SMpro read on a machine whose actual silicon does not have the interface, which is the same class of
// error as keying on the architecture: it reads a possibility as a fact about this machine.
fn smpro_admitted_counts(families: List<SoCFamily>) -> SmproAdmittedCounts {
  let carrying = families |> filter(f => ampere_soc_family_carries_smpro(family: f))
  SmproAdmittedCounts { total: count(families), carrying: count(carrying) }
}

// THE ONE ROSTERED FAMILY THAT CARRIES, when the roster is unanimous about it. Returns the family only
// when EVERY admitted family carries the facility -- unanimous rosters are the only case where the
// compatibility roster settles the question without an installed-silicon observation.
fn smpro_carrying_family(families: List<SoCFamily>) -> SoCFamily? {
  match families |> first() {
    Present { value: lead } =>
      if smpro_roster_is_unanimous(families: families) { Present { value: lead } } else { none }
    Absent => none
  }
}

// UNANIMOUS MEANS EVERY ADMITTED FAMILY CARRIES, or the roster is empty (and the caller answers the
// empty case as Unestablished before it asks). Comparing counts rather than folding a boolean because
// the MIXED case has to be distinguishable from the all-absent case, and a single Bool erases it.
fn smpro_roster_is_unanimous(families: List<SoCFamily>) -> Bool {
  let c = smpro_admitted_counts(families: families)
  c.total > 0 && c.carrying == c.total
}

fn first_soc_family(families: List<SoCFamily>) -> SoCFamily? {
  families |> first()
}

// THE MACHINE'S SMpro FACT AS CARRIED ON THE MANAGED ROW, and the reason it is shaped this way rather
// than as one always-present site standing: an SMpro site is an OBSERVATION OF AN INTERFACE, so it can
// only exist where the interface exists. When the field was a bare SmproSiteStanding (Admitted |
// Refused), an x86 machine could only be represented by FABRICATING an Ampere site -- either claiming a
// reading of an interface it has never had, or claiming a refusal to read one. Both are lies about the
// platform, and the refusal form is the subtler one: "refused" implies the pass existed and was declined.
//
// So the site hangs off the platform verdict and only Carried owes it. That makes "an x86 machine is a
// valid controlled machine" representable at all: it answers NotCarried, carries no site, and still
// binds its BMC control route and stays managed.
type SmproPlatformStanding
  // The platform carries SMpro AND the site was read through a bound controller endpoint. The only arm
  // with an endpoint to join against the secured one, because it is the only arm that claims a read.
  = SmproPlatformCarried { family: SoCFamily, site: SmproSiteStanding }
  // A named family the cited set answers negatively: NO site exists and none is owed. There is no
  // endpoint to compare, because nothing was read and nothing was claimed.
  | SmproPlatformNotCarried { family: SoCFamily }
  // The silicon is unknown -- no family bound, or a compatibility roster that mixes carrying and
  // non-carrying families with no installed-silicon observation to select one. No site, and no claim
  // that the platform lacks the interface either.
  | SmproPlatformUnestablished { reason: NonEmptyStr }

// THE PLATFORM STANDING FOR A MACHINE, from its board's SoC families plus (only where the platform
// carries) the SMpro site standing. Kept as one fn so a consumer never assembles the arms by hand and
// never has to decide what to pass for `site` on a platform that has no interface -- which is exactly
// the decision that produced the fabricated field.
fn smpro_platform_standing(
  access: BmcAccessObservationStanding,
  site: SmproSiteStanding,
) -> SmproPlatformStanding {
  match smpro_facility(access: access) {
    SmproFacilityCarried { family: family } => SmproPlatformCarried { family: family, site: site }
    SmproFacilityNotCarried { family: family } => SmproPlatformNotCarried { family: family }
    SmproFacilityUnestablished { reason: reason } => SmproPlatformUnestablished { reason: reason }
  }
}

// THE SAME VERDICT OVER AN EXPLICIT FAMILY SET, for a witness that asks about silicon without minting a
// board row per question. Mirrors smpro_facility_of_families above it, one arm at a time.
fn smpro_platform_standing_of_families(
  families: List<SoCFamily>,
  site: SmproSiteStanding,
) -> SmproPlatformStanding {
  match smpro_facility_of_families(families: families) {
    SmproFacilityCarried { family: family } => SmproPlatformCarried { family: family, site: site }
    SmproFacilityNotCarried { family: family } => SmproPlatformNotCarried { family: family }
    SmproFacilityUnestablished { reason: reason } => SmproPlatformUnestablished { reason: reason }
  }
}

// WHICH CONTROLLER ENDPOINT, IF ANY, OWES THE SECURED-ENDPOINT JOIN. Only the Carried arm read
// something, so only that arm has an endpoint to hold against the secured one. The old shape asked
// "which host is the SMpro site on?" for EVERY machine, which quietly required every machine to have a
// site -- the requirement that made an x86 managed host impossible.
fn smpro_platform_controller_host(standing: SmproPlatformStanding) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match standing {
    SmproPlatformCarried { family: _, site: site } =>
      match site {
        SmproSiteAdmitted { site: s } => Present { value: s.endpoint.host }
        SmproSiteRefused { cause: _ } => none
      }
    SmproPlatformNotCarried { family: _ } => none
    SmproPlatformUnestablished { reason: _ } => none
  }
}

// WHETHER THE ADMISSION RECORD IS WELL-FORMED ABOUT THE PLATFORM. Carried must present a read site, so
// its endpoint is compared by the caller; NotCarried and Unestablished must present NO site, which is
// what keeps a fabricated Admitted site off an x86 row -- the arms without an interface cannot smuggle
// one in, and the arms with one cannot leave it out.
fn smpro_platform_is_well_formed(standing: SmproPlatformStanding) -> Bool {
  match standing {
    SmproPlatformCarried { family: _, site: site } =>
      match site {
        SmproSiteAdmitted { site: _ } => true
        SmproSiteRefused { cause: _ } => false
      }
    SmproPlatformNotCarried { family: _ } => true
    SmproPlatformUnestablished { reason: _ } => true
  }
}

// WHY NO SMpro PASS SUBJECT EXISTS, in the machine's own words. The old path collapsed
// not-licensed to 'no subject' and the boot diagnostic then rendered 'this host has no bound controller
// to read SMpro through' -- FALSE for a machine whose controller is bound and whose platform simply
// does not carry the interface. Every refusal reason here names the actual cause.
type SmproSubjectOutcome
  // Only a genuine subject: the mint's own refusals are their own arms below, never folded in here.
  = SmproSubjectMinted { subject: SmproPassSubject }
  | SmproSubjectNotCarried { family: SoCFamily }
  | SmproSubjectUnestablished { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | SmproSubjectSiteRefused { cause: SmproSiteRefusal }
  // The site is evidence about a DIFFERENT controller than the bound one: both endpoints survive, since
  // naming them is the whole value of the refusal.
  | SmproSubjectEndpointMismatch { bound: BmcControllerEndpoint, site: BmcControllerEndpoint }

// WHETHER ANY OF THIS MEANS A PASS WAS READ. Used by the boot diagnostic to decide whether to render a
// reading at all.
fn smpro_subject_exists(outcome: SmproSubjectOutcome) -> Bool {
  match outcome {
    SmproSubjectMinted { subject: _ } => true
    _ => false
  }
}

// WHETHER THE ABSENCE IS THE PLATFORM'S FAULT RATHER THAN A BROKEN BINDING: NotCarried and Unestablished
// say 'no such interface / unknown silicon', which is not the same complaint as 'the route is not bound'.
fn smpro_absence_is_platform_cause(outcome: SmproSubjectOutcome) -> Bool {
  match outcome {
    SmproSubjectNotCarried { family: _ } => true
    SmproSubjectUnestablished { reason: _ } => true
    _ => false
  }
}

// THE HUMAN-READABLE CAUSE, so the boot path renders WHY rather than inventing one. Sourced from the
// arms, never assembled from a host name or from the absence of a controller.
fn smpro_subject_absence_reason(outcome: SmproSubjectOutcome) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match outcome {
    SmproSubjectMinted { subject: _ } => "an SMpro pass subject was minted; there is no absence to explain"
    SmproSubjectNotCarried { family: family } =>
      join(["the platform does not carry the SMpro interface: the bound board's SoC family ",
        (family.name as String),
        " is not among the cited SMpro-carrying families, so there is no pass to take"], "") as NonEmptyStr
    SmproSubjectUnestablished { reason: reason } =>
      join(["the platform is not established: ", (reason as String),
        "; no SMpro pass was taken because it is not known whether the silicon has the interface"], "") as NonEmptyStr
    SmproSubjectSiteRefused { cause: cause } =>
      join(["the platform carries SMpro and a controller is bound, but the SMpro site was refused: ",
        smpro_site_refusal_text(cause: cause)], "") as NonEmptyStr
    SmproSubjectEndpointMismatch { bound: bound, site: site } =>
      join(["the platform carries SMpro and a controller is bound, but the SMpro site is evidence about ",
        (site.host as String), ", not the bound controller ", (bound.host as String)], "") as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

// WHY THE SITE ITSELF WAS REFUSED, in words. Named per refusal cause rather than rendered from the
// machine's identity, so the boot path can quote the actual cause.
fn smpro_site_refusal_text(cause: SmproSiteRefusal) -> String {
  match cause {
    SiteHasNoTargets => "the SMpro site declares no targets"
    SiteControlNotATarget { socket: _ } => "the site's control socket is not one of its targets"
    SiteControlAmbiguous { socket: _, matches: _ } => "more than one target claims the control socket"
    SiteSocketRepeated { socket: _ } => "a socket appears twice among the site's targets"
    SiteAddressRepeated { seven_bit_address: _ } => "a 7-bit address appears twice among the site's targets"
  }
}

// WHETHER A STANDING LICENSES AN SMpro READ. Kept separate from the standing so a consumer that only
// gates on it cannot pattern-match its way into treating Unestablished as NotCarried.
fn smpro_facility_licenses_read(standing: SmproFacilityStanding) -> Bool {
  match standing {
    SmproFacilityCarried { family: _ } => true
    SmproFacilityNotCarried { family: _ } => false
    SmproFacilityUnestablished { reason: _ } => false
  }
}

// WHETHER A READING WAS TAKEN. Independent of the facility standing above, by construction: a
// NotTaken reading on a Carried platform is a skipped pass, and on a NotCarried platform there is no
// pass to take.
fn smpro_reading_taken(reading: SmproReading) -> Bool {
  match reading {
    SmproProbed { observation: _ } => true
    SmproNotTaken { reason: _ } => false
  }
}
